Multi-Tenancy in Kubernetes.

Imagine a large apartment building.

Hundreds of families live inside.
Everyone shares the same building...
Same entrance.
Same electricity.
Same water supply.
Same elevators.
But each family has its own apartment.
You can't walk into your neighbor's house.
You can't use their kitchen.
You can't occupy all the parking spaces.
Everyone shares the infrastructure while keeping their own private space.

That's exactly what Multi-Tenancy in Kubernetes is.

The Apartment Building = Kubernetes Cluster.

Instead of giving every team its own Kubernetes cluster (which is expensive), organizations create one large Kubernetes cluster that multiple teams share.
Think of it as one apartment building.
Each Apartment = Namespace.
Every team gets its own Namespace.
For example:
Team A → Namespace: team-a
Team B → Namespace: team-b
Team C → Namespace: team-c
Inside each namespace, teams deploy their own:
Pod, Services, ConfigMaps & Secrets & Persistent Storage.
Their applications stay isolated from everyone else's.

What is Shared?

Although teams are isolated, they still share the underlying infrastructure.
Worker Nodes.
Cluster Networking.
Storage.
Kubernetes Control Plane.
This is why Kubernetes is so resource efficient.

One common interview question which get asked often.

But How Do We Prevent Chaos?

Imagine one tenant:
Uses all the electricity.
Occupies every parking slot.
Walks into other apartments.
The building would become unlivable.
Kubernetes prevents this using multiple layers of isolation.

1. RBAC (Access Control).

Who is allowed to enter which apartment.
Users only get access to their own namespace.

2. Resource Quotas.

Each apartment gets a limited electricity budget.
Every namespace receives limits on:
• CPU
• Memory
• Storage
No single team can consume the whole cluster.

3. Limit Ranges.

Every appliance must stay within safe limits.
Developers don't accidentally create oversized Pods.
Default CPU and Memory limits are enforced automatically.

4. Network Policies

Neighbors cannot walk through each other's walls.
Pods communicate only when explicitly allowed.
Everything else stays isolated.

5. Pod Security Standards

Every apartment follows the building's safety rules.
These policies prevent containers from running with unnecessary privileges or unsafe configurations.

One-line takeaway.
One Kubernetes cluster. Many teams. Shared infrastructure. Isolated workloads.
That's Multi-Tenancy.
